  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nA northwest flow aloft pattern will persist across the Northern \nPlains this weekend into most of next week. A series of weak upper \nlevel waves will pass through this flow leading to a rollercoaster \ntemperature pattern of sorts over the course of the next several \ndays. Sfc high pressure exits east tonight. A fairly healthy \nsoutherly return flow has set up across central SD this afternoon \nand will traverse east tonight. Temperatures will not be quite as \ncool overnight as they were this morning with readings in the mid \n40s to low 50s. South to southwest flow ahead of the first upper \nwave will start us off Saturday. Sfc low pressure and associated \ncold front approach the area by morning. Timing of the fropa closer \nto midday or afternoon should be enough to bump of 850mb temps into \nthe low to mid teens C. This translates into highs in the upper 60s \nto mid 70s. Thickening cloud cover will accompany this fropa along \nwith a 20-50 percent chance of showers. Moisture looks limited so \nexpect hit and miss variety shower where not everyone sees rainfall.\n\nWinds turn northwest behind the front and become gusty up to 25-40 \nmph during the afternoon across central SD and as far east as the \nJames Valley. We cold advect Saturday night as sfc high pressure \nbegins to build in. With the high to our west and northwest most of \nSaturday night into Sunday should help to prevent any significant \ntemperature falls to frosty levels. The weekend will finish on a dry \nand cooler note but with readings still near normal for early \nOctober. The high will exit to our east come Monday and we'll be \nback to strong southerly flow and a warm air advection pattern. \nExpecting temperatures through the early to middle portion of next \nweek to warm back above normal with daytime highs in the 70s. \nTuesday still appears that it will be the warmest day of the next \nseveral with readings in the upper 70s to low 80s.",
